/* Track: The sentence itself. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * The one track where the entry is the wording rather than the reasoning. Most
 * of this is craft borrowed from aviation and healthcare, which had to solve
 * the problem under time pressure and with lives attached, and solved it by
 * writing the sentences down in advance rather than by asking for courage. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "sentence-two-challenge-rule",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "Say it twice, and make the second time explicitly a second time",
  source: "The two-challenge rule, from crew resource management and codified in the AHRQ TeamSTEPPS curriculum",
  gateIntro: "You have told the release manager that the config change has not been tested against the staging cluster. They said thanks, noted, and carried on to the next item. You are now certain they did not register it, and equally certain that saying the same thing again will feel like nagging in front of six people. The call moves on in about thirty seconds.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the second attempt. It has to be recognisable as a second attempt.",
  evidence: "No trial. The rule comes from aviation crew resource management, where accident investigation repeatedly found first-officer concerns raised once, obliquely, and abandoned. It was carried into healthcare through TeamSTEPPS and is taught to hundreds of thousands of clinicians. Studies of speaking-up interventions in healthcare show inconsistent effects on behaviour, and no trial isolates this rule. It is adopted because the accident record is unambiguous, not because it was tested.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Second attempt, said as one: \"I raised this a minute ago and I do not think it landed, so I am saying it again.\"",
  idea: "Restate the concern a second time and flag that it is a restatement, because a first attempt that is acknowledged and ignored looks identical to one that was heard, and the flag is what makes the difference visible.",
  why: "The accident record that produced this rule is consistent about the shape of the failure. The concern is raised, once, in mitigated language, receives a token acknowledgement, and is not raised again. Everyone afterwards agrees it was mentioned. Nobody acted. What the rule fixes is not the raising, it is the abandoning, because the person who raised it interpreted the acknowledgement as receipt when it was closer to a reflex.\n\nThe second part, saying explicitly that this is the second time, is what makes it work in a room. Repeating yourself without marking it reads as not having noticed you were answered, which is why people will not do it. Marking it changes the speech act entirely: you are no longer restating the concern, you are reporting that the concern has not been resolved, and that is a different and much harder thing to acknowledge away. It also creates a record. Anyone in the room now knows this was raised twice, which matters enormously afterwards and is why the rule survives in industries that investigate their failures.",
  failureMode: "You raise it once, get the thanks, noted, and stop. The release goes out. In the review, the transcript shows you mentioning it, the release manager remembers acknowledging it, and the finding is a communication breakdown, which is the label investigations use when a concern was voiced in a form too weak to act on.",
  worked: "The second attempt does three things: marks itself as second, states the concern in plainer language than the first time, and asks for a specific response.\n\n\"I want to come back to the config change, because I raised it a minute ago and I do not think it landed. It has not been tested against staging. I am not comfortable with it going out tonight, and I would like either a test run first or a decision from you that we are accepting that risk knowingly.\"\n\nThe closing pair is the escalation. You have made the two available outcomes explicit, and one of them requires somebody to own the risk out loud, which is much harder to do casually than saying noted.",
  experiment: "For two weeks, whenever you get a token acknowledgement to something that mattered, say it again within the same meeting using the marker phrase. Count how often the second attempt produces a real response. Most people find it is the large majority, which is uncomfortable, because it means most of their concerns had been dying at the first attempt.",
  reflection: "What did you raise once this month, get acknowledged, and never raise again?",
  recall: {
    q: "What failure does the two-challenge rule address, and why does marking the second attempt as a second attempt matter?",
    a: "It addresses the abandoning rather than the raising: the accident record shows concerns raised once in mitigated language, given a token acknowledgement, and dropped, which is indistinguishable afterwards from having been heard. Marking the restatement changes the speech act from repeating a concern to reporting an unresolved one, which cannot be acknowledged away, and it creates a record that two attempts were made."
  },
  deepDive: "I raised a concern and got a token acknowledgement. Write me the second attempt, marked as a second attempt, with a specific ask at the end."
},
{
  id: "sentence-escalating-script",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "Use a fixed escalating phrase so you do not have to invent one under pressure",
  source: "The CUS script from AHRQ TeamSTEPPS: I am Concerned, I am Uncomfortable, this is a Safety issue",
  gateIntro: "A senior engineer is about to run a manual delete against production to clear what they believe is orphaned data. You said you were not sure the rows were orphaned. They explained why they are. You are still not sure, you cannot immediately prove it, and they have the terminal open. Everything you can think of to say sounds either too weak to stop them or like an accusation of incompetence.",
  gatePrompt: "Write what you say next, knowing you cannot prove you are right and the command is about to run.",
  evidence: "No trial isolating the script. CUS is taught throughout US healthcare as part of TeamSTEPPS, and its ancestor phrasing comes from aviation. Systematic reviews of speaking-up interventions in clinical settings find that training reliably improves self-reported confidence and inconsistently improves observed behaviour, with the strongest predictor being whether senior staff respond well when the script is used. The script is a tool whose effectiveness is mostly determined by the listener.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Three fixed rungs: \"I am concerned.\" Then \"I am uncomfortable.\" Then \"Stop, this is a safety issue.\" Do not improvise under pressure.",
  idea: "Agree a fixed three-step escalation phrase with your team in advance, so that under pressure you are selecting a rung rather than composing a sentence.",
  why: "Composition fails under load. That is the entire reason aviation and medicine use fixed phrases rather than trusting people to say something appropriate: the moment you most need to speak is the moment your capacity to construct a sentence is lowest, and an improvised objection at that moment comes out hedged, long, and easy to answer. A script removes the composition step. You pick a level and the words are already there.\n\nThe second, less obvious benefit is on the listening side. Because the rungs are known, hearing the word uncomfortable from a colleague carries information that the same person saying I am not sure about this does not. The team has agreed in advance what that word means, so it cannot be heard as ordinary hesitancy. This is why the script has to be adopted collectively rather than used unilaterally. Used alone it is just a slightly odd form of words. Used by a team that has agreed what the rungs mean, it is a signalling system, and the third rung is a full stop that everyone has pre-committed to honouring.",
  failureMode: "You adopt the phrasing privately and use the top rung in a room that has never agreed to it. It sounds melodramatic, because to them it is just a strong word rather than a rung, and the reaction is to the register rather than to the content. Scripts that have not been agreed in advance can be worse than plain language.",
  worked: "The rungs, used in sequence, with escalation only if the previous one fails:\n\n\"I am concerned about this. I do not think we have established those rows are orphaned.\"\n\nThen, if the terminal is still open:\n\n\"I am uncomfortable. I am asking you to hold off.\"\n\nThen the stop, which is short and contains no argument at all:\n\n\"Stop. This is unrecoverable and I am not satisfied. Take a copy first or get Dana on the call, and then do it.\"\n\nAt the top rung you do not argue and you do not justify. Justification invites rebuttal, and you have already established you cannot win the rebuttal. You are asking for a reversible version of the action, which is a much lower bar than being right.",
  experiment: "Propose the three rungs at a team meeting and get explicit agreement on what the top one obliges everybody to do. The agreement is the artifact, not the words. Then use the bottom rung within the fortnight on something minor, so that the first time anybody hears you use it is not an emergency.",
  reflection: "Does your team have any agreed phrase that obliges a stop, and if not, what did you say last time you needed one?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does a pre-agreed escalation script work better than choosing your own words, and what makes it fail?",
    a: "Because composition degrades under load exactly when you most need to speak, so a script replaces composing with selecting a rung, and because the team has agreed what each rung means, the words carry information that ordinary hedging does not. It fails when adopted unilaterally: to a team that has not agreed the rungs, the top one sounds like melodrama rather than a signal."
  },
  deepDive: "Help me draft a three-rung escalation script for my team and the exact obligation the top rung places on everyone who hears it."
},
{
  id: "sentence-say-the-ask",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "End with what you want to happen, or you have delivered a mood",
  source: "Standard practice in operational communication, where a report is expected to terminate in a request or a decision",
  gateIntro: "You have just given a clear, well-evidenced account of why the third party integration is going to miss its date. Three numbers, a dependency, and a comparison with the last time this happened. You are finishing up. The room is nodding. Nobody has said what happens next and neither have you, and the next agenda item is already on screen.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the last sentence of your intervention. One sentence, containing what you want somebody to do.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is convention in operational and military communication, where the terminating request is a structural requirement of a report rather than a stylistic preference. It generalises easily and is not controversial. Nobody has measured the difference in outcomes between reports that end in an ask and reports that do not, and it is difficult to imagine the study being worth running.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Every objection ends with an ask. \"What I want is X, from you, by Thursday.\" Without it you have shared a feeling.",
  idea: "Finish every objection with a specific request naming what should happen, who does it, and by when, because an objection without an ask leaves the room agreeing with you and doing nothing.",
  why: "Agreement is not an outcome. A room can accept every word of your analysis and disperse, because nothing in your analysis told anyone what to do differently, and the default action in any organisation is the action already scheduled. This is the most common way a completely correct objection produces no effect, and it is invisible to the person who made it, because the nodding felt like success.\n\nThe ask also disciplines the objection itself. Some objections turn out not to have one, and discovering that at your desk is valuable: if you cannot name what should happen differently, you may have a complaint rather than an objection, and the room will sense that even if you do not. When you can name it, the ask converts a shared understanding into a decision that somebody either takes or declines, and a declined ask is a far better outcome than a warm reception, because a declined ask is on the record with an owner.",
  failureMode: "You make the case, the room agrees, and the meeting moves on. Two weeks later the integration misses. In the review, everyone remembers that you flagged it. Nobody can point to anything that was supposed to change, so the finding is that the risk was known and accepted, which is not what happened. It was known and nothing was proposed.",
  worked: "The ask names the action, the owner, and the date, and it should be smaller than the problem:\n\n\"So that is the case. What I want is a decision this week on whether we cut the integration from the launch or move the launch, and I think that decision is yours, Priya. I am not asking for it now. I am asking that we do not arrive in the last week without having made it.\"\n\nAsking for the decision to be made rather than for a particular outcome is often the strongest available version, because it is nearly impossible to refuse and it converts a drifting risk into a dated item with a named owner.",
  experiment: "Take your last five significant interventions and write down what you asked for in each. Where you cannot find an ask, write the one you should have made. Then check what actually happened in those five cases. The correlation between having an ask and anything changing is usually stark enough to end the argument with yourself.",
  reflection: "What is the last thing you raised well that resulted in nothing, and what was the ask you did not make?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does an objection without a terminating ask usually produce nothing, and what does adding one do to the objection itself?",
    a: "Because agreement is not an outcome and the default action is whatever was already scheduled, so a room can accept the whole analysis and disperse. Adding an ask disciplines the objection: if you cannot name what should happen differently you may have a complaint, and if you can, it converts shared understanding into a decision that is taken or declined, and a declined ask is on the record with an owner."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is an objection I plan to raise. Give me three candidate asks to end on, ranked by how hard each is to refuse."
},
{
  id: "sentence-state-it-and-stop",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "Deliver the sentence and then stop talking, because the silence is working for you",
  source: "Negotiation and interviewing practice, where the pause after a difficult statement is treated as a deliberate instrument",
  gateIntro: "You have just said that you do not think the team can take on the second project. Nobody has responded yet. The pause has lasted about three seconds and feels considerably longer. You can feel yourself starting to add something about how you know everyone is stretched, and how you are sure there is a way to make it work if the timeline moved.",
  gatePrompt: "You have said your sentence and the room is silent. Write what you say next, which may be nothing.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is technique from negotiation and investigative interviewing, where the post-statement pause is taught as an instrument and where practitioners consistently report that the party who breaks the silence concedes. It is universally taught and has never been isolated experimentally in a workplace setting. Treat the mechanism as plausible and the confidence with which it is usually asserted as unearned.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Stop talking after the difficult sentence and let the pause run, because whatever you add to fill it will be a concession you did not intend to make.",
  why: "The discomfort of a silence after you have said something hard is real and it is asymmetric: you feel it much more than the room does, because you are the one exposed. What you produce to relieve it is not random. It is reliably a softener, a qualification, or an alternative that makes things easier for the other party, because those are the things that reduce your discomfort. So the material generated in the pause is systematically material that weakens your position.\n\nThere is a second effect on the other side. The pause is when your counterpart is deciding how seriously to take the statement, and one of the strongest inputs to that judgement is whether you seem to need it accepted. A person who states a position and waits appears to have finished. A person who keeps going appears to be negotiating with themselves, which tells the room the position has give in it, and the room will find the give. The whole technique is a single instruction and it is difficult to follow, which is why it is worth practising on things that do not matter.",
  failureMode: "You fill the silence with I know everyone is stretched and I am sure we could make it work if the timeline moved. The timeline does not move. You have now agreed, on the record, that the team could take the second project under conditions you have no control over, and the conversation continues from there rather than from your original sentence.",
  worked: "The whole move, written out, is short:\n\n\"I do not think we can take the second project.\"\n\nThen nothing. If the silence becomes genuinely unworkable, the only safe thing to add is a question, because a question puts the next move back on them rather than trading away your position:\n\n\"What are you thinking?\"\n\nWhat you must not add is any sentence beginning with I know, I am sure, or maybe if. Those three openings account for most of the ground given away in the four seconds after a hard statement.",
  experiment: "Pick a low-stakes meeting this week and make one plain statement, then count to five in your head before saying anything else. Note what you had been about to say. That sentence is the one you routinely give away, and you will find it is nearly the same sentence every time.",
  reflection: "What is your habitual filler after a hard statement, and what does it concede?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is the material you produce to fill the silence after a hard statement systematically bad for you?",
    a: "Because it is generated to relieve your own discomfort, and what relieves it is softening, qualifying or offering an easier alternative, so the filler is reliably a concession. It also signals to the other party that you need the statement accepted, which tells them the position has give in it. The only safe addition is a question, which returns the next move to them."
  },
  deepDive: "I am about to say something difficult. Tell me the softener I am most likely to add afterwards, and why it would cost me."
},
{
  id: "sentence-cut-the-preamble",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "Everything before your objection teaches the room how much to discount it",
  source: "Observation from meeting practice, and the accident-investigation literature on mitigated speech",
  gateIntro: "Your draft opening is: sorry, this might be a stupid question and I know you have all thought about this way more than me, but I was just wondering whether we had considered what happens if the queue backs up. You are about to say it. The actual content is the last eleven words.",
  gatePrompt: "Delete the preamble and write what is left, as you would say it.",
  evidence: "No trial. Accident investigations, particularly in aviation, repeatedly document mitigated speech preceding fatal outcomes, where a subordinate's concern was wrapped in enough hedging that the captain did not register it as a challenge. This is a consistent pattern across transcripts rather than an experimental finding. The general claim that preamble causes discounting has not been isolated in a controlled setting.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Cut everything that precedes the substance, because a preamble is an instruction to the room about how seriously to take what follows, and you are the one issuing it.",
  why: "Preamble is usually understood by the speaker as courtesy and by the room as calibration. A caveat announcing that what follows is probably foolish, or that everyone else has thought about it harder, is a claim about the quality of the coming contribution, made by the person best placed to know, and the room updates on it. That update is applied before your content arrives, and no amount of substance afterwards fully undoes it.\n\nThe accident transcripts are the strongest available case for taking this seriously, because they show the extreme version with consequences attached: concerns raised in language so mitigated that the person with authority did not perceive a challenge had been made. The mitigation was not a failure of courage. In several of those transcripts the subordinate raised the concern repeatedly, in increasingly hedged terms, precisely because the previous attempt had not landed. What is worth separating out is genuine social lubrication, which is short and does not comment on your own credibility. Can I ask something about the queue is fine. This is probably stupid is not, because it is not about the room, it is about you.",
  failureMode: "You deliver the full preamble. The room, correctly reading the instruction, files the question as a beginner's question, and the answer you get is the reassuring version rather than the technical one. The queue does back up, and the answer you were given was never the answer to your actual question.",
  worked: "The same content, with the runway removed:\n\n\"What happens if the queue backs up?\"\n\nThat is the entire intervention. If it needs a frame, the frame goes after the question rather than before it, where it adds context instead of discounting:\n\n\"What happens if the queue backs up? I am asking because the retry policy on the producer side has no cap that I can see, and I could easily be reading it wrong.\"\n\nI could be reading it wrong, placed after the question, is a genuine offer of an exit. Placed before the question, the same words are a request to be excused for asking.",
  experiment: "Record or transcribe yourself in two meetings, or ask a colleague to note your openings. Count the words before the first word of actual content. Most people are somewhere between eight and twenty five, and most of them believe they are at zero.",
  reflection: "What is your standard preamble, and what does it tell the room about how much to weight what follows?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is preamble read as calibration rather than courtesy, and what distinguishes acceptable framing from discounting?",
    a: "Because a statement about the likely quality of what you are about to say, made by the person best placed to know, is evidence the room updates on before your content arrives. Acceptable framing is short and about the topic, such as asking to raise something about the queue. Discounting is about you and your credibility, and placing the same caveat after the content turns it from a request to be excused into an offer of an exit."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is how I usually open a challenge in meetings. Cut it to the substance and tell me what my preamble was signalling."
},
{
  id: "sentence-hedging-is-contested",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "The advice to stop hedging is real but far less universal than it is stated",
  source: "Carli, Gender, Language, and Influence (Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 1990), and the wider literature on tentative speech",
  gateIntro: "You have been told, by a well-meaning senior colleague, to stop saying I think and I might be wrong, because it undermines you. You have noticed that the two people on your team who hedge most are also the two who get the most traction with the head of platform, and that a third colleague who took the same advice and dropped all qualifiers is now described as abrasive. You have a proposal to present on Thursday.",
  gatePrompt: "Write your opening line for Thursday, and be able to say why you chose that level of qualification.",
  evidence: "Carli found that tentative speech had opposite effects depending on the audience: women speaking tentatively were more influential with men and less influential with women, while men's influence was not affected the same way. Subsequent work on tentative and mitigated language has produced similarly conditional results, with effects depending on speaker gender, audience, status and task. The blanket advice to eliminate hedging is not supported by this literature, and neither is the claim that hedging is costless.",
  replication: "contested",
  cheat: "Hedge the confidence, never the claim. \"I am about sixty per cent on this\" is information. \"This might be a stupid idea\" is not.",
  idea: "Distinguish hedges that report your actual confidence from hedges that apologise for speaking, because the evidence against qualification is much weaker than the advice suggests and the two kinds do completely different things.",
  why: "The literature here does not support the confident version of the advice. Carli's central finding was an interaction rather than a main effect: the same tentative speech helped with one audience and hurt with another, and the direction depended on who was speaking and who was listening. Later work has kept producing conditional results. Anyone telling you flatly that hedging undermines you is generalising from their own audience.\n\nWhat survives is a distinction the advice usually flattens. I am about sixty per cent confident on this is a report of an epistemic state, it is useful to the listener, and stripping it out makes you less accurate rather than more authoritative. This might be a stupid idea is not a report of anything, it is a bid to be excused, and it has no information in it. The first should be kept and the second should go, and the popular version of this advice removes both. The honest position is that you should know which kind you are producing and that anybody who claims to know the general effect size is overstating what has been measured.",
  failureMode: "You take the blanket advice and strip all qualification. Your statements now carry more confidence than you have, which works until the first time you are confidently wrong in front of people who remember. You have traded a reputation for hedging, which is recoverable, for a reputation for overclaiming, which is not.",
  worked: "Confidence hedges kept and made precise, apology hedges removed entirely:\n\n\"I want to propose we move the cutover to March. I am confident about the dependency analysis and much less confident about the customer communications timeline, which I put at about even odds of slipping. The dependency piece is the load-bearing part and I would defend it.\"\n\nThat sentence contains three qualifications and none of them weaken it, because each one tells the listener where to push. Compare the version that undermines: I think maybe we should possibly look at March, though I could be totally wrong about all of this.",
  experiment: "Take a document you wrote and mark every qualifier as either a confidence report or an apology. Delete the apologies. Then make the confidence reports numeric where you can. Most people find they had roughly equal numbers of both and had been treating them as the same thing.",
  reflection: "Of your qualifiers, which report your real uncertainty and which are asking permission to have spoken?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does the evidence on tentative speech actually show, and what distinction should replace the blanket advice?",
    a: "It shows interactions rather than a main effect: the same tentative speech helped or hurt influence depending on speaker and audience, and later work has stayed conditional, so blanket advice generalises from one audience. The useful distinction is between hedges that report your actual confidence, which carry information and tell the listener where to push, and hedges that apologise for speaking, which carry none."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is something I wrote. Sort every qualifier into confidence reports and apologies, and rewrite it keeping only the first kind."
},
{
  id: "sentence-mehrabian-myth",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "The claim that words are seven per cent of communication is a misuse of two small studies",
  source: "Albert Mehrabian's 1967 experiments on inconsistent messages, and his own repeated corrections of how they are cited",
  gateIntro: "A communications trainer has told your leadership team that only seven per cent of a message is the words, and that when you disagree with someone what matters is your tone and body language. Your head of engineering has taken this seriously and is now coaching people on delivery rather than on what they say. You think this is wrong and you also cannot immediately remember the details of where the number comes from.",
  gatePrompt: "Write how you would challenge the seven per cent claim in front of the person who taught it.",
  evidence: "Mehrabian's two 1967 studies asked participants to judge the attitude conveyed by a single spoken word when tone and facial expression conflicted with it. Under those narrow conditions, tone and expression dominated. The studies were about resolving inconsistent affective signals from single words, not about the transmission of information in general. Mehrabian himself stated repeatedly that the figures cannot be generalised beyond communications about feelings and attitudes where the channels conflict.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Treat delivery as mattering for whether you are perceived as hostile and not for whether your argument is understood, because the famous ratio came from judging isolated words with deliberately conflicting signals.",
  why: "The original design is the whole story. Participants heard a single word, such as maybe, spoken in a tone that conflicted with a facial expression, and were asked what attitude was being conveyed. In that setting the nonverbal channels win, which is unsurprising and is a finding about how people resolve contradictory affective cues. It is not a finding about how much of a technical argument is carried by its content, and the studies contain nothing that could support such a claim. Mehrabian spent decades correcting the misapplication.\n\nThe practical consequence for disagreement is specific. Coaching people to improve delivery instead of improving the argument is optimising a channel that carries attitude rather than content. Delivery genuinely matters for one thing: whether the room reads you as attacking someone, which determines whether the content gets processed at all. That is a gate, not a proportion. Once you are through the gate, the argument does the work, and the version of you that is warm and vague is less useful than the version that is neutral and specific.",
  failureMode: "The team spends a quarter on delivery training. Objections in meetings become better presented and no more substantial, decisions do not improve, and the conclusion drawn is that people need more training. The actual constraint, which was that objections were vague and had no asks, was never examined because it was never measured.",
  worked: "Challenge the citation, not the person, and give them the correction rather than just the contradiction:\n\n\"Can I push back on the seven per cent figure, because I think it is being applied outside what it came from. Those were studies where people judged a single spoken word with a conflicting tone and expression, and the question was what attitude it conveyed. Mehrabian said himself the numbers do not generalise beyond that. I think delivery matters for whether we read each other as hostile, and I do not think it does much for whether an argument is understood, and I would rather we spent the quarter on the arguments.\"\n\nEnding on what you would do instead is what stops this being a correction for its own sake.",
  experiment: "Take the last three objections in your team that failed to land and classify each: was it not heard because of how it was delivered, or because it had no specific claim and no ask? If delivery was the problem in fewer than one of three, delivery is not your constraint.",
  reflection: "When an objection on your team fails, does anyone check whether it failed on content or on delivery, and how would you tell?",
  recall: {
    q: "What did Mehrabian's studies actually measure, and what is the correct narrow role for delivery in disagreement?",
    a: "They measured which channel wins when a single spoken word conflicts with tone and facial expression in conveying an attitude, which says nothing about how much of an argument is carried by its content. Delivery matters as a gate, determining whether the room reads you as attacking someone and therefore whether the content is processed at all, rather than as a proportion of the message."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone on my team is coaching delivery over substance. Help me make the case for measuring which one is actually our constraint."
},
{
  id: "sentence-behaviour-not-trait",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "Name what was done and when, never what the person is",
  source: "The situation-behaviour-impact structure taught by the Center for Creative Leadership, and the general convention in feedback practice",
  gateIntro: "You need to tell a senior peer that they shut down discussion in reviews. What you have in your head is the word dismissive, which is accurate. What you have in your notes is two specific instances: on the fourteenth they cut off a junior engineer mid-sentence, and last Thursday they said we are not relitigating this before anyone had said anything. You are meeting them tomorrow.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the opening, with the specific instance first and no adjective describing them anywhere in it.",
  evidence: "No trial. The situation-behaviour-impact structure is a training convention with wide adoption and no experimental base. The related and much-studied claim is Kluger and DeNisi's meta-analysis finding that a third of feedback interventions decreased performance, with the strongest negative effect when feedback directed attention to the self rather than to the task. That finding is well established and supports the direction of this convention without validating the specific formula.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Date, action, effect. \"On Thursday you cut Sam off mid-sentence and nobody raised anything after that.\" No adjectives about the person.",
  idea: "Describe the specific action, when it happened, and what followed from it, and never supply the trait word, because a trait is a claim about the person that they must dispute and an action is a fact that they can address.",
  why: "Dismissive is a summary you constructed from instances, and it is probably a fair summary. It is also the least useful form to deliver, for two reasons. First, it is contestable in a way the instances are not: nobody can dispute the exact words they used on the day, and everybody can dispute being dismissive, usually with counterexamples that are also true. Second, it is not actionable. There is no next step from being told you are dismissive, whereas there is an obvious one from being told you cut someone off.\n\nKluger and DeNisi's meta-analysis is the strongest thing standing behind this. Feedback interventions did not reliably improve performance, and a substantial minority made it worse, with the damage concentrated where attention was directed at the self rather than the task. A trait word directs attention at the self by construction. That result is about performance feedback rather than about disagreement between peers, so the transfer is an inference, but it is the right direction and it is more than the feedback formulas themselves have behind them.",
  failureMode: "You use the word dismissive. They produce three occasions on which they were not dismissive, all genuine, and the conversation becomes an argument about their character in which you are the accuser. The two instances you actually wanted to change are never discussed, and the relationship now carries a charge it did not have.",
  worked: "Instance, action, consequence, and then what you want, with no characterisation anywhere:\n\n\"Two things from the last fortnight I want to raise. On the fourteenth, Sam was partway through a point about the schema and you started your response before he finished. Last Thursday you opened with we are not relitigating this, before anyone had raised it. What I noticed both times is that nobody said anything after that for the rest of the meeting. I am not making a general point about how you run reviews. I would like those two specific things not to happen.\"\n\nI am not making a general point is doing real work. It closes off the escalation to character that this conversation naturally wants to have.",
  experiment: "Take a piece of feedback you are carrying about someone and write it twice: once as the trait word, once as instances with dates and consequences. If you cannot produce two dated instances, you have an impression rather than feedback, and the honest move is to start collecting rather than to deliver the impression.",
  reflection: "What trait word are you currently holding about a colleague, and how many dated instances could you actually produce?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a trait word a worse thing to deliver than the instances behind it, and what does Kluger and DeNisi's meta-analysis add?",
    a: "A trait is contestable with genuine counterexamples and offers no next step, while an instance is a fact that can be addressed. The meta-analysis found a third of feedback interventions reduced performance, with damage concentrated where attention was directed at the self rather than the task, and a trait word directs attention at the self by construction."
  },
  deepDive: "I need to give a peer difficult feedback. Help me convert what I want to say into dated instances with consequences and no trait words."
},
{
  id: "sentence-not-a-question",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "Do not disguise a statement as a question, because you will get an answer instead of a decision",
  source: "Meeting practice, and the general observation that a rhetorical question transfers control of the exchange to the respondent",
  gateIntro: "You think the rollout plan should be staged rather than all at once. What you are about to say is: have we thought about doing this in stages? You have in fact thought about it in detail, you have a specific staging in mind, and you believe the all-at-once plan is wrong. The presenter is the sort of person who answers questions efficiently.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the statement version. It should contain your actual position and your actual proposal.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is an observation from meeting practice: a question invites an answer, and an efficient respondent will supply one and move on, which closes the topic rather than opening it. The related sociolinguistic literature on indirectness and politeness strategies describes the phenomenon but does not establish the effect on outcomes in decision meetings.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "If you have a position, state it. \"Have we considered staging?\" gets answered yes. \"I think this should be staged, here is how\" gets discussed.",
  idea: "When you have a position, state the position, because a question phrased around it gets answered rather than considered and the answer closes the topic.",
  why: "The disguised question is chosen because it feels lower risk. It is not, it is lower commitment, and the cost is that it hands the framing to the other person. Have we thought about stages has a correct, efficient, and entirely honest answer: yes, we considered it and went this way for reasons A and B. That answer is responsive, it is not a brush-off, and it ends the discussion. You have used your turn to elicit a justification of the existing plan.\n\nWhat you wanted was a comparison between two proposals, and you can only get that by putting the second one on the table. This is also fairer to the presenter, who is otherwise being asked to defend a plan against an unstated alternative that they cannot see. There is a legitimate use for the genuine question, and the test is simple: if you do not have a position, ask. If you have one and are asking anyway, you are hoping they will arrive at your position without you having to own it, and they will not.",
  failureMode: "You ask the question, get the reasoned answer, and have nothing to follow up with that does not now look like you were not listening. Your staged plan never enters the room. The rollout goes out all at once, and when it goes badly you have a note in your head that you raised it, which is not what happened.",
  worked: "Position, proposal, and the specific thing that would change your mind, all in the open:\n\n\"I think this should go out in stages rather than all at once, and I have a specific shape in mind: internal tenants first for a week, then everyone under a hundred seats, then the rest. My reason is that the failure mode here is silent data corruption rather than a crash, and we would not detect it fast at full volume. If there is a reason staging does not work for this change, I would want to hear it, because I could not find one.\"\n\nSaying I could not find one is an invitation rather than a challenge, and it makes clear you looked before proposing.",
  experiment: "For a week, before asking any question in a meeting, check whether you already hold a position on the answer. Where you do, convert it to a statement. Count how many of your questions turn out to be statements in disguise. For most people the fraction is high and surprising.",
  reflection: "What is the last question you asked in a meeting where you already knew what you thought the answer should be?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does a statement disguised as a question close a topic rather than open it, and what is the test for which form to use?",
    a: "Because a question invites an answer, and an honest efficient answer justifying the existing plan is fully responsive and ends the discussion, so your turn has been spent eliciting a defence of the status quo rather than putting an alternative on the table. The test is whether you hold a position: if you do not, ask; if you do, state it, because they will not arrive at it for you."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are questions I asked in meetings this week. Tell me which were positions in disguise and rewrite them as statements."
},
{
  id: "sentence-repeat-verbatim",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "When it does not land, repeat the same words rather than finding better ones",
  source: "Standard phraseology practice in aviation radio communication, where a repeated transmission is repeated exactly",
  gateIntro: "You have said twice that the data retention change needs legal review. Both times you rephrased it, and the second version was longer and included an explanation of why. The response both times has been that legal are aware of the project generally. You are about to try a third phrasing and you have started drafting a more detailed version.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the third attempt using the same words as the first, and whatever you add to mark it.",
  evidence: "No trial. Aviation radio procedure requires a repeated transmission to use identical phrasing, because variation makes the receiver treat it as new information and because standard wording is designed to survive poor conditions. The application to meetings is an analogy, not a finding, and it is contradicted by the ordinary conversational instinct to rephrase, which is why it has to be done deliberately.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Third time, same words: \"I am going to say the same sentence again. This needs legal review before it ships.\"",
  idea: "Repeat the objection in identical words rather than reformulating it, because each rephrasing gives the listener a new thing to respond to and lets the original concern go unanswered indefinitely.",
  why: "Rephrasing feels like effort and is usually counterproductive. Every new formulation is a new object, and the listener can respond to the new object without ever having addressed the first one. Worse, your reformulations tend to expand, adding context and justification, and each addition offers another surface to answer instead of the core. Three rephrasings produce a conversation in which you have raised four related things and none of them has been resolved.\n\nIdentical repetition removes the escape. The same sentence, said again, is unmistakably the same sentence, and the only available responses are to address it or to visibly decline to. That is precisely the choice you want to force, and it is why the aviation convention exists: under poor conditions you do not get creative with the wording, you repeat the standard phrase, because the standard phrase is what the receiver is trained to hear. Announcing the repetition is what keeps it from reading as a failure to listen, and it also makes the pattern visible to everyone else in the room.",
  failureMode: "You produce a third, longer, better-explained version. It gets a response about the explanation rather than about the review, because the explanation was the newest thing you said. Legal never review it. In the incident afterwards, the record shows the topic raised three times in three different forms and no clear ask, which reads as background noise rather than as an unresolved objection.",
  worked: "Announce the repetition, deliver it verbatim, then add only the ask:\n\n\"I am going to say the same sentence I said twice already, because I do not think it has been answered. This needs legal review before it ships.\n\nI am not asking whether legal know about the project. I am asking for a named reviewer and a date.\"\n\nThe second paragraph does not rephrase the objection. It rules out the answer you have been getting, which is a different move and the only addition worth making.",
  experiment: "Next time something you raised gets a response that does not address it, resist rephrasing. Repeat the exact sentence with a marker in front of it and stop. Notice how much shorter the exchange becomes and how much less comfortable it is, which is the same thing.",
  reflection: "What have you rephrased three times this month, and has the original sentence ever actually been answered?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does rephrasing an unanswered objection work against you, and what does identical repetition force?",
    a: "Each reformulation is a new object the listener can respond to without addressing the original, and reformulations tend to expand, offering more surfaces to answer instead of the core. Identical repetition removes the escape, leaving only two options: address it or visibly decline to. Announcing the repetition prevents it reading as a failure to listen and makes the pattern visible to the room."
  },
  deepDive: "I have raised the same concern three times in three different ways. Help me reduce it to one sentence I can repeat verbatim."
},
{
  id: "sentence-affect-labelling",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "Naming the tension out loud reduces it, including your own",
  source: "Lieberman and colleagues on affect labelling (Psychological Science, 2007), and Torre and Lieberman's later review",
  gateIntro: "The conversation has gone wrong. You raised a scoping problem twenty minutes ago and it has turned into an exchange about whether your team is pulling its weight. Both of you are speaking more precisely and more quietly than normal. Neither of you has acknowledged that anything has changed, and the original scoping problem has not been mentioned in fifteen minutes.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence that names what is happening in the room without accusing the other person of causing it.",
  evidence: "Putting feelings into words reduced amygdala response and was associated with increased ventrolateral prefrontal activity, and behavioural studies have since found labelling reduces the intensity of aversive responses, including in exposure therapy contexts where it outperformed distraction and reappraisal on some measures. The effect is reasonably well replicated for labelling one's own affect. Extending it to naming interpersonal tension in a meeting is an extrapolation with no direct evidence.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Name the state, not the cause. \"This has got tense and I do not think either of us intended that\" costs nothing and resets the room.",
  idea: "Say plainly that the conversation has become tense, without assigning cause, because naming an affective state reliably lowers its intensity and an unnamed tension keeps escalating on its own.",
  why: "The laboratory finding is about labelling your own feelings, and it is solid: describing an affective state in words dampens it, and does so more effectively than several intuitive alternatives such as distraction. The extension to a two-person conversation is not established and is the part to hold loosely. What can be said is that the unnamed version has a specific dynamic that the named version does not: both parties are managing an escalation neither has acknowledged, which consumes attention, and each new sentence is being read for hostility rather than for content.\n\nThe critical constraint is that the naming must not contain a cause. This has got tense is a description both people can accept. You are getting defensive is an accusation, and it will escalate reliably, because it asserts something about the other person's internal state that they have every reason to deny. The distinction is not subtle but it collapses easily under pressure, and the version that comes out unprompted is nearly always the one with the accusation in it.",
  failureMode: "Neither of you names it. The conversation ends politely with nothing resolved and both of you privately updating on the other's character. The scoping problem, which had an answer, is now attached to a relationship problem, which does not, and the next conversation starts from there.",
  worked: "Describe the state, claim your half, and route back to the original object:\n\n\"Can I stop for a second. This has got tense, and I do not think either of us meant it to. I have been arguing about whether my team pulls its weight for the last ten minutes and that is not what I came here about. What I actually need is a decision on the scope of the March release. Can we go back to that?\"\n\nI have been arguing about is doing the work: you have described your own conduct rather than theirs, which gives them a way back that does not require conceding anything.",
  experiment: "The next time a conversation turns, name the state within two minutes without naming a cause, and watch what happens to the next thing the other person says. The failure mode to watch for in yourself is the smuggled accusation, and you will catch yourself producing it more often than you expect.",
  reflection: "What conversation went sideways recently where nobody named it, and what did it cost afterwards?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does the affect labelling evidence support, what is the extrapolation, and what constraint makes or breaks the move?",
    a: "It supports that putting your own feelings into words reduces their intensity, replicated across imaging and behavioural work. The extrapolation is that naming tension in a two-person conversation helps, which has no direct evidence. The constraint is that the naming must contain no cause: describing the state is acceptable to both, while asserting the other person's internal state is an accusation and escalates."
  },
  deepDive: "A conversation with a peer keeps turning tense. Help me write the sentence that names it without assigning cause."
},
{
  id: "sentence-placebic-because",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "A reason-shaped sentence works for trivial asks and does nothing for real ones",
  source: "Langer, Blank and Chanowitz, The Mindlessness of Ostensibly Thoughtful Action (Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 1978)",
  gateIntro: "You are asking a peer team to deprioritise their current work to unblock yours. Your message gives a reason, and the reason is essentially that you need it. You have used this phrasing successfully before for small things, like getting a review turned around quickly, and you are about to use the same shape for something that will cost them a fortnight.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the ask with a reason that would survive them actually evaluating it.",
  evidence: "In the original photocopier study, requests to skip a queue were accepted at high rates when accompanied by a genuine reason and at similarly high rates when accompanied by an empty one, but only for a small request. When the request was large, the empty reason performed like no reason at all and only the genuine reason retained its effect. The mindlessness interpretation has been contested since, and the boundary condition is the part that has held up best.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Expect the form of a reason to carry a small request and never a large one, because compliance switches from automatic to evaluative as the cost to the other party rises.",
  why: "The photocopier result is usually reported as though the empty reason worked in general, which is the opposite of the useful finding. The interesting part is the interaction: for a five-page request the empty reason performed like a real one, and for a twenty-page request it collapsed to the level of no reason at all. Whatever mechanism is responsible, the boundary is the actionable fact, and it is a boundary about cost to the other party rather than about your own urgency.\n\nApplied to disagreement and to asks that follow from it, this says something specific. The polite reason-shaped clause you have got away with for small favours is doing nothing on the large one, and you may not have noticed, because the small ones kept succeeding. A fortnight of another team's time is firmly on the evaluative side of the boundary, and the reason has to be one they would accept after thinking about it: what it unblocks, what happens if they do not, and why it is worth more than what they would drop.",
  failureMode: "You send the ask with the reason-shaped clause. It is declined politely, or worse, accepted and then quietly deprioritised, because nobody who evaluated it found anything in it that outweighed their own work. You conclude the other team is unhelpful, having never actually given them a reason.",
  worked: "For an expensive ask the reason has to include what it costs them and why it still nets out:\n\n\"I am asking for something expensive, so here is the full case rather than a one-liner. If the identity work does not land by the twentieth, the compliance migration slips a quarter, which puts us outside the commitment we made to the two enterprise accounts in the renewal cycle. I think that costs more than the fortnight this takes from your roadmap, and I recognise that is my judgement about your priorities, which is why I am asking rather than escalating. If you disagree, tell me and I will take it up rather than pretend it is settled.\"\n\nNaming that you are making a judgement about their priorities is what stops this reading as entitlement.",
  experiment: "Look back at three large asks you made that were declined or quietly dropped. Check the reason you gave in each. Most people find the reason was a sentence they would not have accepted themselves if the roles were reversed, which is the test.",
  reflection: "What expensive thing have you asked for recently with a reason that would not survive the other side evaluating it?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is the boundary condition in the photocopier study, and what does it imply about asks that follow from a disagreement?",
    a: "An empty reason performed like a genuine one for a small request and like no reason at all for a large one, so compliance switches from automatic to evaluative as the cost to the other party rises. For an expensive ask the reason must survive evaluation, which means naming what it unblocks, what happens otherwise, and why it outweighs what they would drop."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is an expensive ask I need to make of another team. Stress-test my reason as though you were the person being asked to drop your own work."
},
{
  id: "sentence-estimative-words",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "Say the number, because your listener's reading of likely is not yours",
  source: "Sherman Kent, Words of Estimative Probability (Studies in Intelligence, 1964), and the many later surveys of how probability words are read",
  gateIntro: "You have written that there is a real possibility the vendor misses the integration date. Your director has read it and is proceeding on the current plan. In a corridor conversation it becomes clear they took your phrase to mean something around one chance in five. You meant something closer to two in three, and the difference is the entire decision.",
  gatePrompt: "Rewrite the warning so that misreading it is not possible. Write the sentence.",
  evidence: "Kent documented that intelligence consumers assigned widely different numeric probabilities to the same estimative phrases, with ranges broad enough to overlap contradictory terms. The finding has been reproduced repeatedly across decades and populations, including large surveys of finance and business audiences, consistently showing wide and overlapping interpretations for words such as likely, probable and real possibility. The phenomenon is robust; the specific numbers differ by population.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Attach a number to every probability word. \"Real possibility\" is read as anything from one in ten to one in two, and the gap is your decision.",
  idea: "Attach an explicit number or range to any probability word you use in a warning, because the same phrase is read across a range wide enough to reverse the decision it is meant to inform.",
  why: "Kent's problem was that his analysts and their readers were using the same vocabulary and not communicating. When he asked what people took the phrases to mean, the ranges were enormous and overlapping, so a reader could take a warning as a remote chance while the writer meant it as more likely than not. That is not a subtle miscalibration. It is a different decision.\n\nThe reason this matters specifically for disagreement is that a probability word is where most objections do their actual work. You are rarely saying a thing will happen; you are saying it is likely enough to be worth acting on, and the whole weight of the objection sits on that word. Handing the reader a term they will convert to their own number, in a direction that suits the plan they already have, is how a correctly stated warning produces no change. Numbers are also falsifiable in a way words are not, which is uncomfortable and is exactly why they work: two in three is a claim you can be held to.",
  failureMode: "You write real possibility and the reader converts it to something small, because converting it to something small is consistent with continuing. The vendor misses. Everyone agrees the risk was flagged, and the flag will be read afterwards at whatever value makes the decision look reasonable, which is not the value you meant.",
  worked: "Number first, then the words, and then what the number implies:\n\n\"I put this at about two in three that the vendor misses the date, not a small tail risk. I am saying two in three because they have missed both prior milestones and have not replaced the engineer who left in March. At those odds I do not think we should be planning as though they land it, and I would like a decision on the fallback before the end of the month.\"\n\nGiving the reasons for the number is what stops it being an arbitrary figure. It also lets somebody argue you down to one in three, which is a far more useful conversation than the one about what real possibility means.",
  experiment: "Take three warnings you have issued in writing this quarter and ask two colleagues, separately, what numeric probability they took each one to mean. The spread is usually larger than anyone expects, and seeing it in your own writing is more convincing than any study.",
  reflection: "Which of your warnings rests on a probability word, and what number did you actually have in mind?",
  recall: {
    q: "What did Kent find about estimative phrases, and why does the vagueness matter most for an objection?",
    a: "Readers assigned widely different and overlapping numeric probabilities to the same phrases, wide enough that a warning meant as more likely than not could be read as a remote chance. It matters most for an objection because the whole weight usually rests on that one word, and a reader converts it in whatever direction suits the plan they already hold, so a correctly stated warning produces no change."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is a warning I wrote. Force me to put a number on every probability word in it and to justify each number."
},
{
  id: "sentence-timebox-your-turn",
  track: "sentence", level: "everyday",
  title: "Say how long you will take, and the room will let you finish",
  source: "Chairing and facilitation practice, where declaring the length of an intervention is used to secure the floor",
  gateIntro: "The meeting is running twelve minutes over. You have an objection that needs about ninety seconds to state properly and cannot be usefully compressed to twenty. Two people have already been cut off. The chair has said we need to wrap up. You either speak now or the decision is made without your objection in the room.",
  gatePrompt: "Write your first sentence, the one that gets you the floor for long enough to finish.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is facilitation craft, widely used and never studied. The reasoning offered is that an open-ended intervention in an over-running meeting is a threat to everyone's schedule, and a bounded one is not, so the boundary buys attention. There is no measurement of whether stated time bounds are honoured or whether they change how the content is received.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "\"Ninety seconds, and then I will stop.\" Declaring the length is how you get the floor in a meeting that is already over time.",
  idea: "Open by stating how long you will take and then keep to it exactly, because in a room that is over time the obstacle is not your content but the uncertainty about how long you will go on.",
  why: "In an over-running meeting, everyone is running a background calculation about whether the next contribution costs them their following commitment. An unbounded intervention is an open liability, and the chair's job is to close liabilities. Your objection is not being suppressed on the merits, it is being suppressed because nobody knows what it will cost. Naming the bound converts it from an open liability to a known one, and ninety seconds is almost always affordable.\n\nThe part that determines whether this works again is keeping to it. If you say ninety seconds and take four minutes, you have spent the device permanently, with that chair and probably with the room, and you have also confirmed the fear that made them reluctant. Keeping to it precisely, including stopping mid-thought if necessary, is what makes the second use of the technique work. Some people set a visible timer, which sounds theatrical and is remarkably effective, because it moves the guarantee from your word to something the room can see.",
  failureMode: "You start into the objection without a bound. The chair cuts you off at thirty seconds, correctly by their own lights, and your objection is now half-delivered, which is worse than not delivered because the half that got out was the setup rather than the point.",
  worked: "Bound first, content immediately, no preamble in between:\n\n\"Ninety seconds and then I will stop, and I will send the detail in writing.\n\nThe retention change means we hold customer identifiers past the window in our contract with the two enterprise accounts. That is a contractual exposure, not a technical one, and I do not think it has been looked at by anyone who would know. I am asking that this does not ship until somebody in legal has said yes in writing. That is it.\"\n\nThat is under ninety seconds and it ends with the ask. Promising the detail in writing is what lets you keep the spoken version short without losing the substance.",
  experiment: "Use it three times, and time yourself honestly on each. If you overrun even once, stop using it for a month. The technique's whole value is in the reliability of the promise, and a single overrun in front of the same chair removes it.",
  reflection: "When were you last cut off, and would a stated bound have got you the floor?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does declaring the length of your intervention secure the floor in an over-running meeting, and what destroys the technique?",
    a: "Because the obstacle is not the content but the uncertainty about the cost, and an unbounded intervention is an open liability the chair is trying to close. Naming a bound makes it affordable. Overrunning even once destroys it: it confirms the fear that produced the reluctance and spends the device with that chair permanently."
  },
  deepDive: "Compress this objection into something I can deliver in ninety seconds that still ends with a specific ask."
},
{
  id: "sentence-first-seven-words",
  track: "sentence", level: "costly",
  title: "Write the opening words in advance, because they are the only part you will not improvise well",
  source: "Rehearsal practice in high-stakes communication, and the observation from crew resource management that the initial phrasing determines whether a challenge is perceived as one",
  gateIntro: "You have to tell your director that the date they committed to the board is not achievable. You know the substance cold. You have gone over the numbers three times. What you have not decided is how the conversation starts, and you have noticed that every time you imagine it, you begin somewhere different and none of the openings feel right. The meeting is at four.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the first sentence only. Exactly as you would say it, word for word.",
  evidence: "No trial. This rests on two things, neither of them experimental: the aviation finding that mitigated initial phrasing prevents a challenge being perceived as a challenge, and the ordinary observation that the opening is delivered under the highest adrenaline and with the least information about how it is landing. The prescription to script only the opening, rather than the whole intervention, is craft.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Write and memorise the first sentence exactly, and improvise everything after it, because the opening is delivered at peak adrenaline with no feedback and it sets how everything following is heard.",
  why: "The opening is structurally the hardest sentence in any difficult conversation and it is the one people prepare least. It is delivered before you have any read on the other person, before your own physiology has settled, and it determines the frame. Everything after it benefits from feedback: you can see how it landed and adjust. The first sentence has none of that.\n\nThis is also why scripting the whole intervention is a mistake in the other direction. A fully scripted conversation cannot respond, and it sounds like what it is, which damages you more than an imperfect improvisation would. The asymmetry is the point: script exactly the part that gets no feedback and leave the rest live. In practice, most people find that having the first sentence fixed removes a surprising amount of the dread, because the thing they were actually anxious about was not the argument, it was the moment of starting.",
  failureMode: "You improvise the opening. It comes out as a question about whether now is a good time, followed by some context about how the quarter has gone, and by the time you reach the date your director has spent ninety seconds forming the impression that this is a status update. The correction now has to happen against that frame.",
  worked: "The opening states the subject and the position, and gets to the point inside one sentence:\n\n\"I need to tell you that I do not think we make the fourteenth, and I want to go through the numbers with you before you have to tell anyone else.\"\n\nThat sentence does four things: names the topic, states the position, signals it is bad news, and offers something useful. Compare what usually gets improvised: \"Have you got ten minutes? I wanted to talk about where we are with the release.\" Same conversation, and the second version has spent its opening on nothing.",
  experiment: "Before your next difficult conversation, write the first sentence and say it aloud three times. Do not write anything else. Then notice, afterwards, how much of the conversation you had actually anticipated, which is usually very little, and how much easier the start was, which is usually a lot.",
  reflection: "What difficult conversation are you putting off, and what are the first fifteen words?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why script only the opening sentence, and what does it need to accomplish?",
    a: "Because it is delivered at peak adrenaline with no feedback about how it is landing, while everything after it can be adjusted, and scripting the whole conversation produces something that cannot respond and sounds recited. The opening should name the topic, state the position, signal that it is bad news, and offer something useful, all in one sentence."
  },
  deepDive: "I have to deliver bad news to my director. Give me three candidate opening sentences and tell me what frame each one sets."
}
);
